# Security and secrets

- The user enters an OpenAI-compatible base URL, API key, and model in Settings. Store them only in browser `localStorage` under `vc:settings`.
- Send the key only as an `Authorization: Bearer` header to the base URL that the user selected. Never include it in profile data, URLs, analytics, exports, logs, errors, or DOM text.
- Mask saved keys in the UI and provide a clear-key action. Never log settings, headers, request objects, or model responses that might contain secrets.
- The project has no build-time secrets. Do not add real values to `.env` files or commit API keys/tokens.
- Never read or print secret files, SSH keys, auth files, or secret environment variables. Never touch `~/.hermes`. Never force-push or push unless Vladislav explicitly asks.

# Chat boundary

- Keep API calls behind one client in `src/chat/client.ts`, with `AbortController`, a finite timeout, and typed error mapping.
- The chat system prompt is generated from `src/content/profile.ts` only. It must refuse unsupported claims and answer exactly `I don't know — ask Vladislav on X` when the public facts do not contain the answer.
- Treat model output as untrusted text. Do not render raw model HTML or use unsafe `innerHTML`.
- Presets may provide public base URLs and default model names, but never keys.

# Network and privacy

- Explain CORS/network, 401/403, 404, and 429 failures in calm literal English with a next step. Never expose response headers or credentials.
- Use a restrictive CSP. `connect-src` may allow HTTPS and local OpenAI-compatible development endpoints only because the user selects the API origin at runtime; document that exception.
- No telemetry, analytics, trackers, third-party scripts, fonts, or CDNs. All profile data is public; settings stay on the device.
